I've written the Meta-Psychology report to `~/Documents/Coding/ai-jdm-paper/scoping/scratch_journals/metapsych.md`, with headings 1–6 plus the fit assessment.

1. **AI policy:** The journal has no formal AI/LLM policy on its guidelines pages or submission forms, and Linnaeus University Press has none either. The only statement is one sentence in an editorial by the Editor-in-Chief, Rickard Carlsson, in "Meta-Reflections on Meta-Psychology" (2023, https://doi.org/10.15626/MP.2023.4032): "AI innovations in science, and scientific publication are welcome, but that it should be used in an open and transparent manner." Nothing says whether an AI can be an author or where AI use must be disclosed. The journal's pages never mention COPE, so it does not defer to COPE; I could not verify COPE membership because the COPE site blocked the lookup.
2. **Model and requirements:** It is diamond open access with no fee, CC-BY, and author-led copy-editing in LaTeX/Overleaf. Requirements:
   - a PsyArXiv preprint with a fixed statement on page 1;
   - an OSF project holding data, code and materials;
   - preregistration, which the guidelines make mandatory even for secondary data;
   - APA style and the APA reporting standards (JARS);
   - end sections on conflicts of interest and funding, plus CRediT author contributions;
   - a submission form that serves as the cover letter and is public.

   No word or abstract limits are stated. Review is fully open, with named reviewers and no blinding. Statistical staff reproduce all analyses before publication.
3. **Fit (verified DOIs):** The journal has published two anchoring papers by the OpAQ team (Röseler et al.), both confirmed via Crossref:
   - "Measurements of Susceptibility to Anchoring are Unreliable" (2024, https://doi.org/10.15626/mp.2022.3236), an Original Article built on OpAQ data;
   - "Falsifying the Insufficient Adjustment Model" (2025, https://doi.org/10.15626/mp.2024.4137), a File-Drawer Report that formalises and tests an anchoring process model.
4. **Scope risk:** The only section that fits us is Original articles, which is limited to "the topic of meta-psychology". The File-Drawer Report route that the 2025 anchoring paper used is closed to us, because it is for the authors' own unpublished data. The 2023 editorial also says that section is being retired. A purely substantive anchoring and model-comparison paper could therefore be desk-rejected on scope. It fits well if framed meta-scientifically, for example as a case study of AI-conducted, preregistered secondary analysis of open data.
5. **Recommendation:** Email the Editor-in-Chief before submitting, since the guidelines invite this when fit is unclear. Describe the AI's role, the authorship line and the meta-scientific framing. The open review record stays public even after rejection, and the OpAQ authors are likely reviewers.
